        "section": "4.4 Reaction classifications"
      },
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "curator_note": "n-Butane molecular ion: simultaneous cleavage of THREE bonds gives an even-electron ion (C2H3+, vinyl cation) plus an ethyl radical and H2. The central C2-C3 bond breaks (ethyl radical), and the terminal methyl C4 loses two H (1,1) as H2 while the C3=C4 vinyl cation forms. Book draws 3 fishhook marks, one on each cleaved bond: the C2-C3 sigma bond <2,3> and the two C4-H bonds <4,5>,<4,6>. The full balanced flow needs 6 single-electron moves; the 3 extra hooks (second electron of C2-C3 -> C3=C4 pi; second electron of one C4-H -> H-H bond; and the ionisation electron of the other C4-H -> EXTERNAL) are implicit, not drawn. The saturated-alkane molecular ion has no closed integer-bond Lewis form, so the precursor SMILES is the NEUTRAL butane skeleton with charge=1/radical=1 in the species fields (EQ4.7 convention)."
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence_mod_rules",
      "source_id": "mod_rules",
      "target_type": "mechanism",
      "target_id": "IMS4-EQ4.5",
      "support_type": "computationally_supported",
      "locator": {
